WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:19.560
PRESENTER: Today's Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.750
is a world class center
for teaching and research.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:24.300
Faculty, students,
and researchers

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.130
are united in their
goal to advance

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.490
the frontiers of knowledge and
solve contemporary real world

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:35.260
problems, following the vision
laid out for MIT 150 years ago.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.720
But the place
itself has certainly

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:39.510
evolved and flourished
since those early days.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.150
It's hard to imagine how much
the scientific landscape has

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.240
changed from when founder
William Barton Rogers first

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.015
started thinking about a new
kind of Polytechnic Institute.

00:00:53.015 --> 00:00:57.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:02.950
DOUGLAS: William
Barton Rogers was

00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:05.480
born at the beginning
of the 19th century.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.470
And that marks the beginning
of an extraordinary time

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:10.640
in US history.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.760
It's really the
transition that we're

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.840
going to witness over a century
from an agrarian rural country

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.150
into an urban and
industrial country.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.490
SMITH: We're beginning to see
the emergence of large cities,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.330
factories, railroads,
canals, all

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:30.110
the early instruments
of big-time industry

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.320
in the United States.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.875
MINDELL: There's
surveying involved,

00:01:32.875 --> 00:01:36.750
there's mechanical
engineering, there's geology,

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.390
there's all kinds of
civil engineering.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.130
DOUGLAS: The sense
of possibility

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:44.270
of what this new technology
would mean for the country was

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.130
very much in Roger's mind.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:50.687
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:50.687 --> 00:01:53.020
PRESENTER: William Barton
Rogers had a lifelong interest

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.290
in education.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:58.100
And by 1835, he's a professor
at the University of Virginia.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.790
He soon signs on to
lead a geological survey

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:02.020
for the state.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.870
Though he loves the project,
he has a big problem

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.970
finding qualified workers.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.759
DOUGLAS: Roger's
inability to hire workers

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.790
for his survey that combines
scientific knowledge

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:16.210
and the ability to use
technical apparatus

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:20.330
was a great problem to
his way of thinking.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:23.650
He wasn't the only person
that needed an individual

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.760
with that skill.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.830
The world was filled
with new industries,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:34.078
and they all needed people
that combined smarts and skill.

00:02:34.078 --> 00:02:35.620
PRESENTER: Eventually,
Rogers decides

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.770
to leave behind the frustrations
and political turmoil

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.740
he's encountered in
Virginia's slave society

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.068
and moved north to the
vibrant city of Boston.

00:02:44.068 --> 00:02:46.360
SMITH: Boston was one of the
leading commercial centers

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.210
of the country.

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:50.930
And the area surrounding
Boston was, without question,

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:54.790
the most developed industrially
of any state in the Union.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.100
ALLISON: There probably
was not an American city

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.520
that had more of a need
for engineers than Boston.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.710
The city itself was
being transformed

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:04.300
by one of the
greatest engineering

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.990
projects of the 19th century,
the filling in of the Back Bay.

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:10.060
SMITH: And then you have
these reform movements,

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:13.700
temperance movements,
pacifist movements,

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.330
and of course, the famous
antislavery abolitionist

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:18.430
movement-- this was the place.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:21.790
Boston was so radical
in its reform spirit.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.290
ALLISON: The wealth
generated by all

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.740
of this industry, Bostonians
put into various philanthropic

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:30.860
enterprises and [? down ?]
schools, hospitals, libraries,

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.400
museums, various
institutions, which

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.260
benefit the broader community.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.000
SMITH: Rogers writes
in his memoirs

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.300
how much he likes these
so-called enterprising spirit,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.450
and even uses the
word, "knowledge

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.042
seeking spirit" of this area.

00:03:47.042 --> 00:03:47.873
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:47.873 --> 00:03:49.290
PRESENTER: For
decades, Rogers had

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:50.970
been talking with
his brother, Henry,

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.460
about a new kind of
Polytechnic institution.

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.440
MINDELL: At the time,
the ideals of science

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.830
really focused on fundamental
principles, somewhat

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.950
disconnected from the
real problems of industry

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.690
and the people who
worked in industry.

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:08.140
SMITH: They began thinking
about how to incorporate science

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.530
into what they referred
to as the useful arts.

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:13.880
Today, we would call
that technology.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:15.730
ALLISON: It's a
revolutionary idea

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:19.510
that someone will go to
school to get training

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.740
to become an architect, an
engineer, or a scientist.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:26.980
These are typically
occupations that people

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:27.970
would learn by doing.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:32.420
DOUGLAS: This was
experimental from the get-go.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:36.670
Even the word technology
was new at that time.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:39.790
KAISER: He wanted students who
had a grasp of human nature,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:41.728
of basic sciences,
of mathematics far

00:04:41.728 --> 00:04:44.020
beyond the requirements for
making this or that machine

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:44.520
work.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.120
He wanted to train
students who would

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.160
be able to guide the nation
through industrialization,

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.144
not just build the widgets.

00:04:52.144 --> 00:04:52.812
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:52.812 --> 00:04:54.770
MINDELL: Different players
are coming together,

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.895
trying to bring a bunch of
different scientific and

00:04:56.895 --> 00:04:59.030
practical institutions together.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.500
And Rogers proves very skilled
at taking that set of people

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.050
and orienting their ideas
toward his proposal.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.440
DOUGLAS: They knew it was
going to be in Boston,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.380
and the land in Back Bay
was the place to put it.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.130
So they were going to have
to convince the Massachusetts

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.550
state legislature
that not only was

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.450
the school worth establishing,
but that it was worth

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:25.115
designating a piece of land for.

00:05:25.115 --> 00:05:27.740
MINDELL: The proposal was bought
to the state of Massachusetts.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.060
And the first step is to
essentially incorporate it

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:32.480
as a state corporation.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.610
And that's what we celebrate on
the founding day of April 10,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:41.186
1861, when the governor
finally signs the MIT charter.

00:05:41.186 --> 00:05:44.102
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:44.102 --> 00:05:48.476
[EXPLOSIONS]

00:05:48.476 --> 00:05:52.364
[WAR DRUMS]

00:05:52.364 --> 00:05:53.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.950
DOUGLAS: Just two days
after MIT's founding

00:05:56.950 --> 00:06:00.790
on April 10 in 1861,
the first shots

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:04.880
are fired on Fort Sumter
in South Carolina.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.120
And with the start
of the Civil War,

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.880
it meant that classes at MIT
could not start right away.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.940
This proved fortuitous
for MIT because it

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:20.320
allowed them the opportunity
to raise additional funds

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.630
to acquire the land to start
construction to hire faculty.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.700
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:30.460
ALLISON: One can imagine
that when Rogers tried

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:34.240
to raise money, it's war
time and energies are

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:36.820
being devoted to other things.

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:38.770
Yet, on the other
hand, if we have

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:40.690
optimism about the
outcome of the war,

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:44.050
we can see the necessity
for young men and women

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.150
to become engineers
and scientists

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.580
to be able to solve
these other problems.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.480
MINDELL: When the moral act is
passed in Washington in 1862,

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.850
it grants every state
some land that then they

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:59.080
can either use or sell to found
an agricultural or mechanical

00:06:59.080 --> 00:06:59.763
institution.

00:06:59.763 --> 00:07:01.180
DOUGLAS: Rogers
and his colleagues

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.060
won support from the
state legislature

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.900
that a portion of the funds
from the Land-Grant Act

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.970
would be dedicated
to this new school,

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:15.050
assuming that they could raise
the other funds successfully.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:18.310
And then they had to
go out house by house,

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:21.970
factory to factory, and
convince people one at a time

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.870
to donate funds to
support this enterprise.

00:07:28.342 --> 00:07:30.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:33.200
PRESENTER: As the war
is ending in 1865,

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.180
the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.670
holds its first
classes in rented space

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:40.330
in downtown Boston's
Mercantile Building,

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:44.340
while constructing its own
buildings near Copley Square.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.680
Rogers' vision for a
new kind of education

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.990
with its emphasis
on hands on learning

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:52.650
made it crucial to establish
cutting edge laboratories.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.050
KAISER: Rogers had a very
strong reaction against what

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.700
he considered rote learning.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.550
And so from the beginning,
MIT was a great innovator

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:02.130
in getting laboratory work
right down to the earliest

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.330
levels of the curriculum.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:04.830
Entry undergraduate
students would

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.080
be entitled to do a lot of
work in the laboratories

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:08.880
with their own hands, not
just seeing something else

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:09.960
demonstrate some effect.

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.360
DOUGLAS: Rogers
needed faculty who

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.850
were going to be
willing to invent

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:18.330
a new kind of curriculum,
that they were going

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:21.840
to have to cobble together
for the first time ever

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.910
laboratory exercises.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:28.410
SMITH: He allowed his professors
to basically experiment

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:32.340
with it, tinker with it, adjusts
it, and build the program.

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:36.659
I think of pickering in physics,
for example, Professor Storer,

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:39.419
who was one of the
early chemists at MIT.

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.580
And both of them
become very famous.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.650
And they're producing
textbooks to accompany

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:46.562
the lab-oriented
educational process.

00:08:46.562 --> 00:08:49.020
MINDELL: If you look at the
curriculum offered in that very

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:53.010
first set of classes
at MIT in 1865,

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:55.710
it looks a lot like what
we call the GIRs today--

00:08:55.710 --> 00:08:58.380
the General Institute
Requirements-- that still,

00:08:58.380 --> 00:08:59.880
every freshman has to take--

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.280
mathematics, chemistry, physics.

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:05.100
Those were all required
at MIT from day one.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:07.860
KAISER: There was an emphasis
on combining basic science

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:09.850
with applied things
in the field.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:13.110
Students took field trips to
all kinds of working places,

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.780
where with the technological
world was being built.

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:17.460
And Barton Rogers
wanted all that right

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.450
in the curriculum for
his undergraduates,

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:19.360
right from the start.

00:09:24.607 --> 00:09:27.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:30.870
PRESENTER: As a young startup,
MIT had its share of hurdles--

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.330
ongoing money troubles,
takeover attempts

00:09:33.330 --> 00:09:35.190
by neighboring Harvard.

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:37.860
But with every passing year with
every successful student who

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.140
went on to make his or
her mark in the world,

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.380
MIT's reputation grew
and the school's standing

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:46.080
became more and more secure.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.810
By 1894, President
Francis Amasa Walker

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.510
was able to declare
in his annual report

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:54.160
that the battle of the
new education is won.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.780
Proclaiming that the influence
of MIT and its innovative ways

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:00.122
are now recognized far and wide.

00:10:00.122 --> 00:10:01.830
HASTINGS: In the early
years, the MIT way

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:03.788
of doing business with
a great deal of emphasis

00:10:03.788 --> 00:10:07.320
on hands on and doing
things in reality

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:10.470
contrasted very substantially
with a more classically

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:11.850
oriented education.

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:15.120
With today, even for
those institutions,

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.550
which are more classically
oriented liberal arts,

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:21.714
they have moved
actually towards MIT.

00:10:21.714 --> 00:10:23.045
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:10:23.045 --> 00:10:24.420
PRESENTER: By the
time operations

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:26.340
moved across the
Charles to the brand

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:30.540
new Cambridge campus in
1916, MIT's ongoing future

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:32.200
seemed assured.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:35.580
Over the decades, the roadmap
Rogers laid out for his school

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:37.260
has proved flexible
enough to stay

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.800
true to his founding ideals,
while incorporating new fields

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:42.117
as they emerge.

00:10:42.117 --> 00:10:43.950
SMITH: There have been
a lot of continuities

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:45.750
in the history of
MIT, especially

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.270
around the type of
curriculum that students

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.880
are required to follow.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.130
There's surely a greater
range of choice today,

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:57.840
but the emphasis on combining
science with practice

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:02.130
is still an important
dimension of what is happening

00:11:02.130 --> 00:11:03.660
around the Institute today.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.430
HASTINGS: That idea
of mind and hands--

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:09.150
Mens et Manus-- that goes right
back to the beginnings of MIT

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:12.720
and its coincident with the
idea that the learning takes

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:15.750
place by doing, as
well as just by seeing.

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.140
DOUGLAS: MIT today shows
remarkable commitment

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:22.320
to the original vision
articulated by William Barton

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:27.270
Rogers of a place that solves
great problems, that educates

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.840
students who have
the capacity to be

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:32.970
independent in their thinking.

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:38.670
Those commitments are timeless
and stretch across 150 years.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:51.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

